        "instruction": "Add diced onion, diced carrot, and diced celery to the pot with the rendered pancetta fat. Season with a pinch of salt to help draw moisture from the vegetables and speed up cooking. Crank the heat a bit and cook until the vegetables are very soft and their color is intensifying — about 3–4 minutes.",
        "duration": "4 min",
        "tips": "Carrot and celery belong in bolognese — they give a well-rounded base of vegetable aromatics. Salt helps draw out moisture so the vegetables cook down faster and caramelize better.",
        "timestamp": 240
      },
      {
        "number": 5,
        "instruction": "Add 3 tablespoons of Mutti brand double-concentrated tomato paste directly on top of the soft vegetables. Cook the paste without the vegetables for 1–2 minutes, letting the oil in the pot fry the paste at a higher temperature than it could reach once liquid is added. This concentrates the tomato flavor.",
        "duration": "2 min",
        "tips": "The tomato paste MUST go on top of the vegetables before any liquid — it needs to cook out at high temperature. Once liquid goes in, the temperature drops and the paste loses that flavor development. Don't skip this step.",
        "timestamp": 360
      },
      {
        "number": 6,
        "instruction": "Add 1 cup of dry red wine to the pot. Let it simmer and reduce until the wine has cooked down and the alcohol has burned off — about 5 minutes. You should see the fat starting to separate from the liquid slightly.",
        "duration": "5 min",
        "tips": "The wine adds acidity and concentrated flavor. Don't rush this — you need the alcohol to cook off completely or it will taste raw. Watch for the fat to rise and separate; that's your signal the wine has cooked down enough.",
        "timestamp": 420
      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "instruction": "Stir in 1 can (28 oz) crushed San Marzano tomatoes, breaking up any large pieces as they go in. Add the browned meatballs back to the pot along with 1 cup low-sodium chicken broth and 1 cup whole milk. Stir to combine.",
        "duration": "3 min",
        "tips": "The milk is key — it mellows the acidity of the tomatoes and adds creaminess. For lasagna specifically, use a bit more tomato than you would for straight bolognese; lasagna needs that tomato-forward flavor.",
        "timestamp": 480
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "instruction": "Return the sauce to a gentle simmer. Bring a cover (or foil) slightly ajar over the pot. Place the entire pot into a 225°F oven for 3 hours, stirring occasionally. The sauce should bubble very gently — never an active boil.",
        "duration": "180 min",
        "tips": "Low and slow is the secret. The meatballs will break down from balls into shreds as they braise. Mash them lightly at the 1.5–2 hour mark to integrate the solid and liquid elements. The sauce will freeze beautifully for months if made ahead.",
        "timestamp": 492
      },
      {
        "number": 9,
        "instruction": "While the sauce braises, make the béchamel: Melt 6 tablespoons of butter in a medium saucepan over medium heat. Once fully melted and foaming, add 7 tablespoons of all-purpose flour. Whisk constantly for 1–2 minutes to toast the flour and develop flavor — it should smell toasty and look lightly golden.",
        "duration": "3 min",
        "tips": "Toasting the flour is not optional — it adds an incredible depth of flavor. Watch for the roux to go from raw-flour-smelling to nutty and toasted.",
        "timestamp": 510
      },
      {
        "number": 10,
        "instruction": "Bring 3 cups of whole milk to a boil in a separate pot. Slowly pour the hot milk into the roux while whisking vigorously to avoid lumps. Increase the heat to medium-high. The starch must come to a boil to fully thicken — keep whisking until it boils and thickens, about 3–5 minutes.",
        "duration": "5 min",
        "tips": "Hot milk into a roux (not cold) prevents lumps. Whisk aggressively as the milk goes in. There's no risk of scorching milk when it comes to a full boil, so crank the heat to speed thickening. The sauce should coat the back of a spoon.",
        "timestamp": 558
      },
      {
        "number": 11,
        "instruction": "Remove the béchamel from heat. Stir in 4 oz grated Parmigiano-Reggiano, 1/2 teaspoon freshly grated nutmeg, and a pinch of cayenne pepper. Season with salt and white pepper to taste. The béchamel should be smooth, creamy, and lightly spiced.",
        "duration": "2 min",
        "tips": "Nutmeg with béchamel is traditional — it adds warmth. Cayenne is optional but it 'wakes up' the sauce with a subtle lift, not spice. Don't oversalt — you'll be layering with salty ragù and cheese.",
        "timestamp": 608
      },
      {
        "number": 12,
        "instruction": "Bring a large pot of salted water to a boil. Add lasagna sheets (such as Barilla d'Orzecho or similar wider, pre-cut sheets) one at a time, stirring gently so they don't stick together. Cook until flexible but still slightly firm — about 3 minutes.",
        "duration": "5 min",
        "tips": "Add sheets one by one and agitate as they go in to prevent sticking — this is the trickiest part of lasagna noodles. Don't overcook; they'll finish cooking in the oven. If a couple stick together, don't panic — just separate them gently and move on.",
        "timestamp": 618
      },
      {
        "number": 13,
        "instruction": "Remove the cooked pasta sheets and lay them flat between pieces of parchment or wax paper so they don't stick together. Don't stack them. Let them cool slightly so they're easy to handle.",
        "duration": "3 min",
        "tips": "Parchment or wax paper prevents sticking. Don't leave them in the water or they'll keep absorbing it and become mushy. They should be pliable but have a slight snap when bent.",
        "timestamp": 663
      },
      {
        "number": 14,
        "instruction": "Preheat your oven to 375°F. Lightly oil a 9×13-inch (or similar) baking dish so the layers don't stick. Spread a thin layer of ragù on the bottom of the dish.",
        "duration": "2 min",
        "tips": "A light coat of oil prevents sticking and helps you get clean slices. Start with just ragù — not béchamel — on the very bottom.",
        "timestamp": 720
      },
      {
        "number": 15,
        "instruction": "Layer in this order: pasta sheet(s), ragù (about 1/4 of the total), béchamel (about 1/4 of the total), Parmigiano-Reggiano (a light sprinkle). Repeat this pattern 4–5 times, varying the direction of the pasta sheets with each layer for structural integrity — alternate horizontal/vertical or crisscross.",
        "duration": "8 min",
        "tips": "Aim for 5 thin layers rather than 3 thick ones — thin layers integrate better during baking and cook more evenly. Stagger the pasta sheets (change direction) so each piece creates an interlocking architecture. Don't oversaturate any layer or the lasagna will be soupy.",
        "timestamp": 762
      },
      {
        "number": 16,
        "instruction": "End with a final layer of pasta sheets, topped with a generous layer of béchamel (not ragù), and a final sprinkle of Parmigiano-Reggiano. The béchamel on top allows the pasta to crisp and turn slightly brown and crackly.",
        "duration": "2 min",
        "tips": "Béchamel on the very top is crucial — it's what allows the exposed pasta to crisp up and get slightly crunchy, which adds incredible texture contrast. This is the magic that separates great lasagna from good lasagna.",
        "timestamp": 842
      },
      {
        "number": 17,
        "instruction": "Cover the baking dish loosely with foil (or parchment) and bake at 375°F for 25–30 minutes until the lasagna is bubbling gently around the edges.",
        "duration": "28 min",
        "tips": "Covering with foil traps steam and prevents the top from browning too fast before the interior heats through. Look for gentle bubbles at the edges — this signals everything is hot.",
        "timestamp": 900
      },
      {
        "number": 18,
        "instruction": "Remove the foil. Increase the oven temperature to 425°F and bake uncovered for 10–15 minutes until the top is golden brown and the pasta edges are slightly crispy.",
        "duration": "13 min",
        "tips": "This final high-heat blast crisps the top layer and gives you those crunchy, golden pasta edges. Watch it — it can brown quickly at this temperature.",
        "timestamp": 1080
      },
      {
        "number": 19,
        "instruction": "Remove the lasagna from the oven and let it rest for 10 minutes before serving. Do not skip this step.",
        "duration": "10 min",
        "tips": "Resting allows all the liquid to rebalance and redistribute throughout the lasagna. This gives you clean, neat slices instead of a soupy mess. The internal temperature continues to cook slightly during rest, so pull it out when it's bubbling actively.",
        "timestamp": 1230
      },
      {
        "number": 20,
        "instruction": "Cut the lasagna into portions and serve. A corner piece is traditional and holds together best. Optionally finish with a pinch of flaky salt and fresh basil.",
        "duration": null,
        "tips": "Corner pieces are structural winners — they hold together best. The lasagna should be rich, velvety, and creamy. If it looks dry, you underseasoned or overcooked slightly; if it's soupy, you layered too generously. Both are minor tweaks for next time.",
        "timestamp": 920
      }
    ]
  }
}
